Depressive disorder in youth
Young Adult & Adolecence
Pervasive depressed/irritable mood, Anhedonia, Declining school performance, Pervasive fatigue

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-009
Reason for Visit: Acute behavioral withdrawal, psychomotor retardation, and expression of passive suicidal ideation requiring immediate psychiatric risk assessment.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Both Parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a rapidly deteriorating adolescent who had become entirely bedbound, refusing food, and expressing profound feelings of worthlessness and a desire not to wake up. An urgent after-hours physical and mental state examination was critical to formally assess acute suicide risk, exclude organic medical causes of profound fatigue, and establish a safe environmental containment plan prior to the availability of daytime adolescent mental health services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "Everything is entirely pointless, I am exhausted all the time, and I just want to go to sleep and never wake up again."
History of Present Illness: Over the preceding four weeks, the patient has exhibited a pervasive depressed and severely irritable mood most of the day, nearly every day. In the last 72 hours, this has precipitously worsened into severe psychomotor retardation observed by the parents, with the patient refusing to leave the bed or engage in personal hygiene. There is markedly diminished interest or pleasure in all previously enjoyed activities (anhedonia). The parents note a significant unintentional weight loss. The patient reports pervasive fatigue, feelings of excessive, inappropriate guilt over minor past events, and a diminished ability to think, directly leading to a sharply declining school performance. The urgent visit was prompted by the patient tearfully expressing recurrent thoughts of death to a sibling this evening.
Functional Status: Currently bedbound; dependent on parents for encouragement to perform basic activities of daily living.
Social/Safety Concerns: High acute risk for self-harm; intense environmental stress on the family unit; complete cessation of academic and social functioning.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 68 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 55 Kg
General: Patient demonstrates a slumped posture, poor eye contact, blunted affect, and significant delayed speech latency. Unkempt appearance.
Physical Exam: Comprehensive neurological examination is grossly intact with no focal deficits. Thyroid gland is not palpable. No stigmata of chronic substance abuse or toxidrome. Mental State Examination (MSE) reveals a depressed, restricted affect, slow and monotonous speech, and morbid thought content, but denies active intent or a specific plan for self-harm at this exact moment. The clinical presentation firmly establishes the diagnostic tetrad of a pervasive depressed/irritable mood, anhedonia, declining school performance, and pervasive fatigue.
Environmental Observations: Patient's bedroom is dark with curtains drawn; medication cabinets in the house have not yet been secured.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Major Depressive Disorder in Youth, Severe, without psychotic features - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Hypothyroidism - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of physical signs such as bradycardia, cold intolerance, dry skin, or a palpable goiter, though baseline TFTs are ordered to definitively exclude.

Substance-Induced Mood Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely based on a negative targeted history, absence of physical stigmata of substance use, and lack of physiological withdrawal signs during the clinical examination.

Adjustment Disorder with Depressed Mood - Ruled out/unlikely due to the sheer severity of the symptoms (psychomotor retardation, pervasive weight loss, passive suicidal ideation) which far exceed an expected reaction to a psychosocial stressor.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Requires 24-hour parental supervision)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is experiencing a severe, acute exacerbation of a major depressive episode, characterized by profound psychomotor retardation, vegetative symptoms (weight loss, insomnia/hypersomnia), and passive suicidal ideation. The sudden verbalization of a desire to die necessitated an immediate, after-hours psychiatric risk assessment to determine if involuntary emergency department transfer was required. Clinical assessment confirmed high distress but no immediate, structured plan or active intent for self-harm, allowing for safe community management provided strict environmental controls were implemented immediately. Delaying this assessment would have left an unassessed, high-risk adolescent in a vulnerable environment, posing an unacceptable risk of a fatal self-harm event.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Conducted a comprehensive safety and risk assessment. Mandated immediate environmental lockdown by parents (securing all sharps, medications, and household chemicals). Ordered baseline bloods (FBC, TFTs, Iron studies, Vit D) to be done routinely. Provided the PHQ-9A to be completed prior to the GP follow-up.
Positive Behavioral Change: To rebuild a basic circadian rhythm, open the bedroom curtains immediately upon waking, utilizing natural light to actively suppress melatonin and improve daytime alertness. To combat severe anhedonia, complete one simple, non-demanding task daily (e.g., showering), explicitly separating the action from the feeling, proving that physical movement is possible even when motivation is absent.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Immediate referral to the local Adolescent Mental Health triage team.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory, urgent review by the regular GP tomorrow morning to initiate a formal Mental Health Treatment Plan and escalate psychiatric referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient expresses an active plan to end their life, attempts any self-harm behavior, or if the parents feel they can no longer guarantee the patient's safety at home, they must immediately drive the patient to the Emergency Department or call 000.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a severe depressive episode requiring multi-disciplinary psychiatric care; recovery is non-linear and requires time.
Clinical Course: The acute crisis is contained, but the underlying neurochemical depression will require ongoing therapy and likely pharmacological intervention initiated by a specialist.

